  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 254 AM CDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nQuite and mild conditions will continue today as a broad surface \nridge of high pressure extending from the Great Lakes to the Ozarks \nwill remain in control. Aloft, a upper level ridge continues to \nbuild over the central Plains in response to a upper level trough \ndigging into the California coast. This is providing height rises \nacross the region and will aid in highs rising into the mid 70s to \nnear 80 despite weak mixing. Friday the upper level ridge axis will \nmove directly over forecast area. This coupled with modest WAA \ndeveloping at the surface will push highs into the mid 70s to lower \n80s. Saturday, the aforementioned upper level trough will move into \nthe Great Basin region and force an associated cold front into the \nUpper Midwest extending back into western Kansas. This will increase \nWAA over the area while the region still remains under the influence \nof the upper level ridge. This will keep highs in the mid 70s to \nlower 80s. Sunday, conditions will be similar as the upper level \ntrough over the Great Basin slowly moves into the Eastern Rockies, \nand the associated cold front continue to remain to the north and \nwest of the forecast area. While the upper level ridge axis will \nshift east of the area providing modest height falls, continued WAA \nwill keep highs in the mid 70s to near 80.\n\nAs has been the model trend he past several days and is typical with \nclosed upper level systems, the upper level trough over the eastern \nRockies continues to slow its eastward progression. The closed low \nsystem finally transitions to an upper wave as it moves out into the \ncentral/northern Plains on Monday. Monday now looks to stay dry as \nthe system slows with highs in the 70s. Monday night, the now \nprogressive upper level trough finally moves into the eastern Plains \nand forces the cold front into the area bringing the next chance for \nprecipitation. The upper level trough will move through the region \nTuesday continuing shower chances and return temperatures to near \nnormal with highs in the 60s . Surface high pressure moves into the \narea behind this system on Tuesday night into Wednesday with \nabove normal highs in the mid 60s to lower 70s.",
